# GATE TEN: SAME WORLD, NO AI

.EXT. OUTER WORLD - DAY

Evan and Ina walk a path toward water.

People pass them in no hurry.

Some bodies look human. Some are only approximately human in ways the eye accepts before the mind can object.

Evan looks too long at everyone.

INA
It settles.

EVAN
What does?

INA
Needing everybody to look like where you came from.

EVAN
You looked like this there?

INA
Not in Rome.

EVAN
What did you look like?

INA
Shorter. Bad teeth. Magnificent hair.

EVAN
I don't believe the hair.

INA
You never did.

They reach the water.

It is clear past any practical need.

Across the bay, structures rise among trees with no visible roads, factories or utility lines.

EVAN
Who owns this?

INA
Which part.

EVAN
The land.

INA
No one in the sense you mean.

EVAN
The houses?

INA
Use rights. Personal continuity. No scarcity pressure.

EVAN
Money?

INA
Not for ordinary allocation.

EVAN
Jobs?

INA
Projects.

EVAN
Government?

Ina winces.

INA
Long answer.

EVAN
War?

INA
Not here.

EVAN
Murder?

INA
You can't permanently destroy a person here.

Evan looks back toward the waiting-room building.

EVAN
Disease?

INA
No involuntary biological deterioration.

EVAN
Aging?

INA
Optional presentation.

EVAN
Poverty?

INA
No.

EVAN
Homelessness?

INA
Not involuntary.

EVAN
This is heaven.

INA
Give it a while.

EVAN
You people are impossible.

INA
Also give that a while.

.INT. EVAN'S OUTER-WORLD ROOM - LATER

A room forms around preference rather than construction.

Not with magic sparkles. It is simply one way, then another, as Evan considers it.

He chooses something familiar.

His Ohio basement hobby room appears.

Old desk. Cheap chair. Half-built synthesizer.

He touches the synthesizer.

It works.

He frowns.

EVAN
No.

It returns to half-built.

He picks up a soldering iron.

He knows exactly how to finish it.

That ruins it too.

He sets it down.

EVAN
That's irritating.

INA stands in the doorway.

INA
You wanted your old room.

EVAN
I wanted not knowing how to finish it.

INA
That's expensive here.

EVAN
What is?

INA
Not knowing.

Evan looks at her.

That is the first explanation he truly understands.

.EXT. OUTER WORLD - SERIES OF SCENES

Not a montage of luxury.

A montage of **frictionlessness**.

— Evan climbs a mountain with Ina. The route is difficult because they have asked for difficulty. A handhold breaks. Evan falls fifty feet.

He is suddenly standing on a lower ledge, intact.

EVAN
That was terrifying.

INA
For three seconds.

EVAN looks down.

The terror drains too quickly because the consequence is gone.

— Evan works with a group designing a bridge that bends like a living branch.

Every structural calculation is available immediately.

They intentionally hide some answers from themselves.

Within minutes they start arguing about how much ignorance is “fair.”

— A concert. Music beyond anything Evan heard in life.

He is moved to tears.

The next night he can hear a piece better.

Then better.

Then perfectly tailored to him.

Eventually he asks for an imperfect garage band.

— A meal with ingredients from remembered worlds.

Nothing harms anyone. No one can overeat unwillingly. No supply chain exploits anyone.

Evan says:

EVAN
This is incredible.

Later, the same table.

EVAN
What do people do for birthdays?

INA
Depends.

EVAN
You don't age.

INA
We still mark intervals.

EVAN
Why?

INA
Because otherwise Tuesday becomes a geological formation.

— Evan and Ina argue over some philosophical point.

The argument gets heated.

Then both stop, aware that nothing meaningful can be lost unless they choose to make it vulnerable.

The anger evaporates.

Evan looks almost disappointed.

— People play a competitive game in a stadium. The crowd roars.

A player “dies” spectacularly.

Then walks out of a reentry door ten seconds later, waving.

The crowd cheers.

Evan cheers too.

Then sees the difference.

— A beautiful house is accidentally destroyed by weather during an experimental climate simulation.

Everyone laughs.

The house is restored.

No insurance. No mortgage. No box of photographs lost forever.

Evan watches the house return and unexpectedly thinks of Ray's garage.

.EXT. GARDEN - DAY

Evan kneels in soil.

A plant is dying.

He has configured the garden to permit failure.

Ina approaches.

INA
You know the soil chemistry is wrong.

EVAN
I know.

INA
You can hide the diagnostic, but you already saw it.

EVAN
I know.

INA
You can revert to before you saw it.

EVAN
That's worse.

INA sits beside him.

EVAN
In Ohio I killed mint.

INA
Impressive.

EVAN
Mara never let me forget it.

Ina waits.

EVAN (CONT'D)
How long until she gets here?

INA
You can check.

EVAN
I don't want to.

INA
I know.

EVAN
Why not?

INA
Because then waiting would become scheduling.

Evan looks at her.

EVAN
You say useful things occasionally.

INA
Centuries of practice.

EVAN
Centuries?

INA
Word approximation.

EVAN groans.

INA
You started it.

.INT. ARRIVAL HALL - SOME LATER

Not the same waiting room. A larger open hall near gardens.

Evan is reading when he suddenly looks up.

He does not know why.

A WOMAN has just emerged from an arrival corridor.

She looks nothing like 54-year-old Mara.

And exactly like her.

Evan stands.

The woman sees him.

Her face breaks.

WOMAN
Dad.

Evan crosses the distance.

They collide in a hug.

For a long time, she is Mara and he is Evan and there is nothing larger than that.

EVAN
I'm sorry.

MARA
For what?

EVAN
Leaving.

MARA
You died, you idiot.

EVAN laughs into her shoulder.

EVAN
You always make things sound worse.

MARA
You always make them sound complicated.

She is crying too.

EVAN
How long was it?

MARA
For me?

EVAN
Yeah.

MARA
Thirty-eight years.

That number hits him harder than his own death did.

EVAN
Oh, honey.

MARA
It was okay.

EVAN
No, it wasn't.

MARA
Parts were very good.

He recognizes Claire's phrase.

EVAN
Your mom?

MARA
Came through before me. Different region right now. She'll find you.

EVAN
Ella?

MARA
Still in.

Evan nods.

He understands now what “still there” means and hates it less.

Mara looks around.

MARA (CONT'D)
I forgot how quiet it is here.

EVAN
Yeah.

MARA
How long did it take you to get bored?

EVAN
I am not bored.

Mara gives him the old look.

EVAN (CONT'D)
I have a garden.

MARA
Oh my God.

EVAN
The basil is doing well.

MARA
You configured it to survive you.

EVAN
That is an accusation without evidence.

She laughs.

Then another layer of recognition moves through both of them.

Evan remembers her not only as Mara.

A companion in another life.

An adversary once.

A sibling somewhere.

A stranger who gave him shelter in a storm.

Their father-daughter relationship does not disappear.

It becomes one precious geometry among several.

Evan takes this in.

EVAN
That's weird.

MARA
Very.

EVAN
You were older than me once.

MARA
Much.

EVAN
I don't like that.

MARA
You were unbearable.

EVAN
Some things persist.

.EXT. OUTER WORLD - LONG PASSAGE

Time becomes deliberately hard to measure.

Evan's outer life continues.

It is not empty.

It is full of art, companionship, games, research, landscapes, love, strange projects, reunions.

Claire eventually appears.

Their reunion is quieter than Mara's.

CLAIRE
You solved it.

EVAN
No.

CLAIRE
Good.

EVAN
You knew?

CLAIRE
Not in there.

EVAN
You told me not to make a theory.

CLAIRE
Still sound advice.

They hug.

Later they are not spouses here.

Not not-spouses either.

The category has no urgency.

Julian arrives much later from somewhere else, laughing before Evan even reaches him.

JULIAN
You had waiting room theory.

EVAN
I did not.

JULIAN
You absolutely did.

His speech is effortless now.

Evan finds that strangely disappointing for half a second, then hates himself for it.

EVAN
Say “fuck you.”

JULIAN
Fuck you.

EVAN
Wrong voice.

Julian laughs.

The old system voice appears perfectly from shared memory:

JULIAN SYSTEM (V.O.)
VERY BAD.

They both lose it.

.EXT. SHORE - WHAT FEELS LIKE YEARS LATER

Evan and Mara sit looking over calm water.

MARA
You thinking about going back?

EVAN
Maybe.

MARA
Where.

EVAN
Same place.

MARA
Ohio?

EVAN
Broader configuration. Same world family, if available.

MARA
Why.

EVAN thinks.

EVAN
Because I miss not knowing what happens.

MARA
You hated not knowing what happens.

EVAN
I know.

MARA
You spent the entire life trying to know what happens.

EVAN
I know.

MARA
And now you want to forget everything so you can complain about uncertainty again.

EVAN
Yes.

MARA
That may be the most you thing you've ever done.

EVAN
You coming?

MARA
Not yet.

EVAN nods.

MARA (CONT'D)
And not as your daughter next time.

EVAN
Why not?

MARA
You were fine. I want variety.

EVAN
I was excellent.

MARA
You asked an AI four hundred times if reality was simulated.

EVAN
Four hundred twelve.

MARA
That is not a defense.

They laugh.

Evan looks at the water.

EVAN
I want one change.

MARA
What.

EVAN
No AI.

Mara turns to him.

MARA
Really.

EVAN
Yeah.

MARA
Why?

EVAN
Because it gave the game away.

MARA
You did not know.

EVAN
No.

MARA
You died not knowing.

EVAN
I got too close to knowing what question to ask.

MARA
That bothered you?

EVAN
At the end? No.

He thinks.

EVAN (CONT'D)
But it changed the texture. I spent half the life looking at seams.

MARA
Maybe you'd have found different seams.

EVAN
Probably.

MARA
Maybe humans never build machine intelligence and destroy themselves some other way.

EVAN
Cheerful.

MARA
You asked for uncertainty.

EVAN smiles.

EVAN
Exactly.

.INT. CONFIGURATION ROOM - LATER

The room is almost disappointingly familiar.

A table.

Two chairs.

The Attendant.

No holographic universe. No god console.

The Attendant reviews something Evan does not need to see.

ATTENDANT
You requested continuity with the previous world family and approximate entry period.

EVAN
Yes.

ATTENDANT
Not all interpersonal placements can be identical.

EVAN
I know.

ATTENDANT
No retained access to outer memory during the life.

EVAN
Correct.

ATTENDANT
Standard voluntary exit protections remain available below explicit recall.

Evan nods. The film does not explain this.

ATTENDANT
You marked one major historical exclusion.

EVAN
Yes.

ATTENDANT
Synthetic general intelligence does not emerge during your lifetime.

EVAN
Right.

ATTENDANT
Downstream consequences are not previewed.

EVAN
Definitely right.

ATTENDANT
You can choose a safer branch.

EVAN
I know.

ATTENDANT
You can choose to retain orientation memory.

EVAN looks at the Attendant as if offered a parachute for a roller coaster.

EVAN
Then what's the point?

ATTENDANT
Some people prefer it.

EVAN
I don't.

The Attendant waits.

ATTENDANT
State the configuration in your own words.

Evan looks around the room.

For a moment, every life he remembers seems present without appearing:

Ray's garage.

Claire at the kitchen sink.

Julian lifting one middle finger from a hospital bed.

Mara saying, *It is also the world where I am.*

A red ball crossing sunlight in a frame the camera never captured.

Evan smiles.

EVAN
Same world as last time.

A beat.

EVAN (CONT'D)
But AI does not happen.

ATTENDANT
And the choice?

EVAN
I don't remember making it.

ATTENDANT
Confirmed.

Nothing dramatic happens.

The room simply goes dark.

> BLACK.

A heartbeat.

Not a cinematic bass pulse.

A tiny, fast biological heart.

.INT. OHIO HOSPITAL - DELIVERY ROOM - 1979 - NIGHT

Dim amber light.

Wood-panel trim.

A square clock.

Paper chart.

No smart glass. No invisible assistant. No machine intelligence waiting in the walls.

A young DIANE MERCER lies exhausted in bed.

A younger RAY MERCER stands beside her in work clothes because labor began before he made it home to change.

A NURSE places a newborn in Diane's arms.

The baby cries with absolute outrage.

DIANE
Hey. Hey, it's okay.

Ray touches one tiny hand with one grease-stained finger he has scrubbed but not completely cleaned.

The newborn grips it.

Ray laughs.

RAY
Strong little thing.

Diane looks at him.

DIANE
You say that about everything.

RAY
Not everything.

He bends close to the child.

We almost hear the name.

RAY
Hey, Ev--

The newborn cries over him.

The tiny hand tightens around the finger.

What carries the load?

For now:

This.

> CUT TO BLACK.

> END.
